FLAC 0.1b2 - FLAC sounds for every Amiga
========================================

  1. What it is
  2. Installation
  3. Playing and saving
  4. Making FLAC files for the Amiga
  5. Speed and memory
  6. Tested
  7. Building
  8. History
  9. License and author


  1. WHAT IT IS
  -------------

  FLAC (the Free Lossless Audio Codec, xiph.org/flac) is the most
  used format for music without any loss: about half the size of
  plain 16-bit sound, and every sample comes back exactly as it was
  recorded.

  flac.datatype teaches the Amiga to play it, in every program
  that plays sounds through datatypes: MultiView, a double-click
  on Workbench, file managers, AmigaGuide, browsers. It is a
  subclass of sound.datatype with its own decoder, one build for
  every Amiga from a 68000 up, with OS 3.0 or later, about 14 KB.

  A FLAC file is recognised by its first bytes ("fLaC"), whatever
  it is called. Files with ID3 tags in front of that (some taggers
  write them into FLAC files) are recognised too.


  2. INSTALLATION
  ---------------

  Copy three files:

      Classes/DataTypes/flac.datatype  to SYS:Classes/DataTypes/
      Devs/DataTypes/FLAC              to DEVS:DataTypes/
      Devs/DataTypes/FLAC-ID3          to DEVS:DataTypes/

  FLAC-ID3 is for FLAC files with ID3 tags in front; it looks past
  the tags and leaves MP3s and other files with ID3 tags alone.

  Then restart the Amiga. (A first install also works with
  AddDataTypes REFRESH, but the system keeps an older descriptor
  it already knows until a restart.)

  To remove it, delete the three files and restart.


  3. PLAYING AND SAVING
  ---------------------

  Double-click a FLAC file's icon, or open it in MultiView (or any
  other program that plays sounds through datatypes).

  Every kind of FLAC file plays: 4 to 24 bits a sample, 1 to 8
  channels, any rate, every encoder setting (block sizes 16 to
  65,535, prediction up to order 32, fixed and variable block
  sizes).

  What you hear depends on the system's sound.datatype:

  - OS 3.5, 3.9 and 3.2 (sound.datatype 44 and later): stereo
    stays stereo. A file with more than two channels plays its
    first two, front left and right.
  - OS 3.0 and 3.1 (sound.datatype 40): no stereo there, so all
    the channels are mixed into one.

  Paula, the Amiga's sound chip, plays at most about 28 kHz. A
  faster file (44.1 or 48 kHz) is averaged down to half the rate,
  or a third or a quarter for even faster ones, while it is
  decoded, so it plays at the right speed and pitch instead of
  slow. Paula plays 8-bit sound: the samples are rounded to 8 bits.

  Title and artist: the file's TITLE and ARTIST tags become the
  sound's name (MultiView's window title) and author. Characters
  that Latin-1 has are kept; others become ?.

  Saving: MultiView's Project > Save As writes the sound as an
  8SVX file, with the right rate and length in its header (OS 3.5
  and later).

  Not played:
  - files that do not say their length (some streamed recordings)
  - files over 24 bits a sample
  - Ogg FLAC (.oga)

  Broken files: every frame is checked (a CRC-8 over its header and
  a CRC-16 over the whole frame) before its samples count. A
  damaged frame is left out (silence there) and the next one is
  found. A file that only says it is long cannot make the datatype
  ask for more memory than the file could hold.


  4. MAKING FLAC FILES FOR THE AMIGA
  ----------------------------------

  Any FLAC file plays. For slow Amigas (see section 5) two things
  make a big difference:

  - 22,050 Hz instead of 44.1 kHz: Paula cannot play more anyway,
    and there are half as many samples to decode.
  - Fewer predictors: flac -2 and below use only the fixed
    predictors, which need no multiplies.

  On a PC, with ffmpeg and the flac command:

      ffmpeg -i song.flac -ar 22050 song.wav
      flac -3 song.wav

  (Add -ac 1 to the ffmpeg line for mono, half again.)


  5. SPEED AND MEMORY
  -------------------

  Like every sound datatype, flac.datatype decodes the whole sound
  before it starts to play, so the time is waiting time before the
  sound starts. Every sample has to be decoded, at any playback
  rate.

  Opening a sound, MultiView's own start left out, on the real
  A1200 (PiStorm with Emu68, OS 3.2.3):

    Sound                                      Time
    60 s, 44.1 kHz stereo, 16 bits (flac -5)   1.24 s
    10 s, 44.1 kHz stereo, 16 bits (flac -8)   0.18 s
    5 s, 48 kHz stereo, 24 bits (flac -8)      0.18 s
    5 s, 22 kHz mono, 16 bits (flac -5)        0.02 s

  Not yet measured on a real 68000, 68020 or 68030. In FS-UAE (an
  emulator, not real hardware) a 14 MHz 68020 decoded about 26,000
  samples a second a channel and a 7 MHz 68000 about 5,000: 10 s
  of CD sound took about 17 s to open on the 68020, and 24 s of
  22 kHz stereo almost 6 minutes on the 68000. On such Amigas use
  short sounds, made as in section 4.

  The bit reader's inner loop is hand-written 68000 assembly, and
  the predictor only ever multiplies 16 by 16 bits (muls.w), also
  for 24-bit sound: no 32-bit or 64-bit multiply calls.

  Memory. The file is read through a small window (the largest
  frame there can be and 4 KB more: about 24 KB for CD sound) and
  decoded straight into the 8-bit sound. Only that is held: a
  minute of 44.1 kHz stereo becomes about 2.6 MB in stereo, half
  that in mono. Cover pictures and other metadata are skipped.


  6. TESTED
  ---------

  - The decoder, on the PC: every sample compared with ffmpeg's
    decoder on 612 files from the flac command and ffmpeg's own
    encoder (levels 0-8, block sizes 16 to 65,535, prediction up
    to order 32, 4 to 24 bits, 1 to 8 channels, 8 kHz to 655 kHz,
    variable blocks, ID3 tags in front), and 1,600 damaged copies
    that must only come through. With the address and
    undefined-behaviour checkers on.
  - The Amiga build's full samples against the PC's: 126 of those
    files on OS 3.2.1 (FS-UAE), every sample the same.
  - Through datatypes: eight test sounds (16 and 24 bits, mono,
    stereo, six channels, tags and a cover picture, a damaged one,
    a minute long) opened as MultiView does and their 8-bit
    samples compared with the PC's, byte for byte: OS 3.2.1 on a
    68020 and a 68000 A600 with OS 3.1 (both FS-UAE), and the real
    A1200 (Emu68, OS 3.2.3). Opening and closing three times loses
    no memory.
  - 0.1b2: a file just past the size where 0.1b1 cut it short,
    files with ID3 tags, and an MP3 with ID3 tags (not taken), on
    OS 3.2.1, 3.1 and a 68000 A600 (FS-UAE). Music played and
    recorded on a 68000, a 68020 and a 68030.


  7. BUILDING
  -----------

  With bebbo's m68k-amigaos-gcc: make in the flac folder gives
  build/flac.datatype, build/FLAC and build/FLAC-ID3. make check
  compares the decoder with ffmpeg's on the PC (needs ffmpeg and
  flac; makes about 2,200 test files).


  8. HISTORY
  ----------

  0.1b2 (21.10.2026)
    Big files are no longer cut short: a sanity limit on the
    length overflowed for some files over about 10 MB (with very
    large blocks, from 2.6 MB) and could cut the sound to a
    fraction of a second. FLAC files with ID3 tags in front are
    recognised (the new FLAC-ID3 descriptor) and played.

  0.1b1 (17.10.2026)
    The first version: a sound.datatype subclass with its own
    decoder, stereo on sound.datatype 44 and later, mono on
    3.0/3.1, sound above 28 kHz averaged down, the bit reader in
    68000 assembly, every frame checked.


